<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Cherusci</h1><p class="st-meta">53 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Germanic people of Arminius, whose ambush of three Roman legions in the Teutoburg Forest in 9 CE ended Rome's plan to rule Germany to the Elbe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Cherusci lived in the region of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> foothills. Caesar mentions them in 53 BCE as neighbours of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a>. After the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a> from 12 BCE they became Roman allies, and their noble sons, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, served in the Roman army and received Roman citizenship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cheruscan society was led by noble families competing for power and Roman favour; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>' rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segestes">Segestes</a> stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 9 CE Arminius led a coalition that destroyed the legions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>. He then fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> to a standstill in 15 to 16 and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> in 17, before being murdered by kinsmen around 21.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Internal feuding weakened the Cherusci; in 47 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>' nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italicus-cheruscan-king">Italicus</a> to rule them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, writing around 98, describes them as worn down and overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a>. They disappear soon afterwards, probably absorbed into the peoples who later formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cherusci.jpg" alt="Cherusci" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Rhine_10BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>53 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arminius, Segestes, Italicus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Weser-Rhine Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd></dl></aside></div>

